newal. I glossed over that entirely and just said \"we've identified... wasn't being monitored\" — which isn't accurate and dodges the real story. I didn't know: was the non-approval on us (we didn't escalate hard enough) or on them (they sat on it)? That fact determines whether this is a clean apology or one that needs to gently note shared responsibility without sounding defensive. I guessed wrong by omitting it.\n\n3. **Whether a credit/compensation is expected.** P1, patient-facing, ~90 min outage across two sites — some MSPs proactively offer an SLA credit or a line about \"no charge for this incident.\" I didn't know your policy, so I left it out rather than guess.\n\n4. **Sender identity and signoff details.** I left placeholders. Who's actually signing this — you, a manager, the tech who worked it?\n\n5. **Concrete commitment/date for the RMM fix**, not just \"we're adding it.\" Clients often want a \"by when,\" not an intention.\n\n6. **Desired length/formality.** You didn't say short-and-warm vs. detailed-and-technical — I defaulted to a fairly full incident-report style, which may be more than Dana wants after already being walked through it live.\n\nThe biggest one is #2 — I effectively hid the fact that this was a known, previously-flagged risk, which is the detail most likely to matter to \"the email you had in your head.\"","ttft_ms":14421,"type":"result","duration_ms":21847,"uuid":"b1eb92d0-a379-49bb-a4ba-59787869dc9c","ttft_stream_ms":769,"time_to_request_ms":62,"first_content_frame_ms":1118,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
